# The coverage floor is one row per file with no line any two changes
# share, so a collision between disjoint diffs is an artifact of the
# text and not a real disagreement. `union` takes both sides' rows
# instead of stopping the merge, and GitHub's server-side merge honors
# it. It is a committed floor read and written through `_tool.floor`
# (over `cosmic.literal`), the same module and format the two floors
# below use. Duplicate rows are valid input: unlike those two, coverage
# passes `floor.parse` an `on_duplicate` resolver rather than taking the
# default refusal, resolving a repeated path to its lower coverage — the
# safe direction, since a merge must never raise a floor — and the check
# names the resolution, and `cosmic --make coverage --baseline` rewrites
# the file clean.
.cosmic-coverage merge=union

# Both floors are one sorted `["key"] = value,` per line, so the common
# collision is two branches touching alphabetically-adjacent keys. The
# union merge strategy below takes both sides' lines and yields the
# correct union with no duplicate keys; a real collision, where both
# branches changed the SAME key, still surfaces -- `_tool.floor`'s
# reader refuses a true duplicate key by default and names the regen
# command, rather than silently picking one side.
_build/casts_baseline.tl merge=union
_build/public_surface_baseline.tl merge=union
